{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2)-(-2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=-t\\,i+({t}^{2}+1)\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2}{}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{((-1)^{2}+(2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2|}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2}+4t)}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{4})}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(1+{t}^{2}+{t}^{4})}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(2t+{t}^{4})}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{},\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 0-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 1|}{{((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because cosine is positive for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{({sin}^{3}⁡t+1)}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{}{\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-{t}^{-1},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={t}^{-2},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-{t}^{-1}\\cdot 0-1\\cdot ({t}^{-2})}{{((-{t}^{-1})^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}}{{({t}^{-2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot ({t}^{2})^{3/2}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot |t{|}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot {t}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-t}{\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-t"] ["math" "t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+{2}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2{e}^{t}|}{{({e}^{2t}+4)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t}+4)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-64901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1+t)^{2},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+t)^{2},\\,y(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2(1+t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2(1+t)\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 2|}{{(\\,(2(1+t))^{2}+(2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8{((1+t)^{2}+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{((1+(0))^{2}+(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1+0)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{2t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}|}{{((2{e}^{2t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{t}}{{(4{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{6{e}^{(0)}}{{(4{e}^{4(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=2{e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{t}\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{t}|}{{((2{e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{3t}}{{(4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-35190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{50}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{27}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-34549" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c7d2aa88073322f9165567e16364afd08109f29f2586e0afafe627298fa33c2"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "q-162534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd7cbf25cba2edc248a0169720c5f4452723f904d7e7dcaf85b402c4835324c8"} {"question" "e-3454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{ (1+\\cos^2 t)^3 } }, \\quad t\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3454.json" "file_sha256" "1eaf79ee260f8d742697aae779d666434164fdb5430c944a8ee847c8e9be4924"} {"question" "e-3454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3454.json" "file_sha256" "1eaf79ee260f8d742697aae779d666434164fdb5430c944a8ee847c8e9be4924"} {"question" "e-3452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of curvature for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t) =  t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3452.json" "file_sha256" "3710502ea5e9b031486db045204c8bbb79969362cf7e8780e117caa02ea1c0e5"} {"question" "e-3452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3452.json" "file_sha256" "3710502ea5e9b031486db045204c8bbb79969362cf7e8780e117caa02ea1c0e5"} {"question" "e-3453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3453.json" "file_sha256" "d95b69c481623fc3b5f76f60b16a2ba4d735ad727035c50876b1461119f7dd93"} {"question" "e-3453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {|y''(x)|} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6x|}{{(1+9{x}^{4})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)|}{{(1+9(-1)^{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6|}{{(1+9)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{10}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069322/example-3453.json" "file_sha256" "d95b69c481623fc3b5f76f60b16a2ba4d735ad727035c50876b1461119f7dd93"}] "usage" {"q-64901" [] "e-3453" [] "e-3454" [] "e-3452" [] "q-34549" [] "q-35190" []} "basis" 2360 "retractions" [[17592186120426 :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩$ at $t = 0$."] [17592186120307 :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y = 2x^{3} - x^{2} + 1$ at $x = 0$."] [17592186120280 :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = 2\\ln (x - 1)$ at $x = 2$."] [17592186119506 :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n$k (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+\\cos^2 t)^3}}, \\quad t\\neq 0$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186119506 :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(t) &= (1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - 0 \\cdot \\cos t)/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-\\sin t)/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-sin ⁡ t)/(\\sqrt{(1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119504 :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $\\mathbf r(t) = t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2$."] [17592186119504 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {\\mid x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t) \\mid} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (\\mid 1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - \\cos t \\cdot 0\\begin{vmatrix})/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid \\sin t\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(\\frac{π}{2}) &= (\\mid \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\mid)/((1 + \\cos^{2} (\\frac{π}{2}))^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/((1 + 0^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(\\frac{π}{2}) &= (1)/(κ(\\frac{π}{2})) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119505 :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1$."] [17592186119505 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {\\mid y''(x) \\mid} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2}, \\mid y^{″}(x)|= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(x) &= (\\mid 6x\\begin{vmatrix})/([1 + (3x^{2})^{2}]^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid 6x\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9x^{4})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(-1) &= (\\mid 6(-1)\\begin{vmatrix})/((1 + 9(-1)^{4})^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid - 6\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10^{3/2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118156 17592186118157 17592186118158 17592186118159 17592186118160 17592186118318 17592186118319 17592186118320 17592186118321 17592186118322 17592186119032 17592186119033 17592186119034 17592186119035 17592186119036] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118155 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118155 :answer-field/id] [17592186118155 :answer-field/key] [17592186118155 :answer-field/type] [17592186118317 :answer